Guard vlc_getProxyUrl against uninitialised JNI fields on Android.

VLC's Android vlc_getProxyUrl() fetches the system proxy via the cached JNI
references fields.System.clazz / fields.System.getProperty. Those are only
initialised by JNI_OnLoad, which the Android dynamic loader calls for a
System.loadLibrary() load but NOT for a plain dlopen() -- which is exactly how a
.NET P/Invoke host resolves libvlc (by library name, no Java bootstrap). With
the fields left NULL, the CallStaticObjectMethod() calls dereference a NULL
class/methodID and the process dies with SIGSEGV the first time any network
access module (http/https/ftp/rtp/...) is opened.

Skip the Android system-proxy lookup (report "no proxy") when those fields are
not initialised, so libvlc's native transport works under a non-JNI host.
---
--- a/src/android/specific.c
+++ b/src/android/specific.c
@@ -346,6 +346,14 @@ char *vlc_getProxyUrl(const char *url)
     JNIEnv *env;
     bool b_detach;
     char *psz_ret = NULL;
     const char *psz_host = NULL, *psz_port = NULL;
     jstring jhost = NULL, jport = NULL;
 
+    /* fields.System is only initialised by JNI_OnLoad, which Android invokes for
+     * System.loadLibrary() but NOT for a plain dlopen() (e.g. a .NET P/Invoke
+     * host that resolves libvlc by name). Without it the CallStaticObjectMethod
+     * calls below dereference a NULL class/methodID and crash (SIGSEGV). Skip the
+     * Android system-proxy lookup in that case and report "no proxy". */
+    if (fields.System.clazz == NULL || fields.System.getProperty == NULL)
+        return NULL;
+
     env = get_env(&b_detach);
     if (env == NULL)
         return NULL;
